.Dd October 14, 2026
.Dt XFT_TIMING 3
.Os xft
.Sh NAME
.Nm xft_get_nanos
.Nd monotonic time in nanoseconds
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In timing.h
.Ft t_u64a
.Fn xft_get_nanos void
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn xft_get_nanos
returns the time elapsed since an unspecified, per-process epoch, in
nanoseconds.
The value is monotonic and never decreases within a single process.
It is not wall-clock time and cannot be compared across processes.
.Ss Types
The header
.In timing.h
includes
.In types/timing_types.h ,
which defines the time value structure and, on x86_64, a calibration
record:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
/* freestanding, or any build on Windows */
typedef struct s_timespec {
	t_i64a	tv_sec;
	t_i64a	tv_nsec;
} t_timespec;

/* libc on Linux */
# include <time.h>
typedef struct timespec t_timespec;

/* x86_64 only */
typedef struct s_tsc {
	t_i64a	base;
	t_i64a	mult;
} t_tsc;
.Ed
.Pp
.Vt t_timespec
follows
.Vt struct timespec
on hosted Linux builds and is a hand-rolled equivalent everywhere else.
.Vt t_tsc
holds the offset and multiplier used to convert the cycle counter into
nanoseconds; it is only defined on x86_64.
.Ss Implementations
The implementation differs by target.
.Bl -tag -width "x86_64"
.It aarch64, freestanding
Calls
.Fn xft_clock_gettime ,
which stores the monotonic time in a
.Vt t_timespec ,
and combines the fields into nanoseconds.
.It x86_64
Calibrates the cycle counter on the first call by sampling
.Fn xft_rdtsc
around a ten-millisecond sleep on
.Fn xft_clock_gettime .
The ratio of elapsed nanoseconds to elapsed cycles is stored in a
.Vt t_tsc ,
and every call thereafter returns the elapsed cycles since calibration
scaled by that ratio.
The calibration state is held in two function-local statics; the first call
performs the calibration, later calls read the counter and multiply.
.It Any other target
Calls the C library's
.Fn clock_gettime
with
.Dv CLOCK_MONOTONIC
and combines the fields.
.El
.Pp
.Fn xft_rdtsc
reads the cycle counter.
It is declared in the private header
.Pa src/internal/xft_p_time.h
and is not part of the public API; the only public function on this page is
.Fn xft_get_nanos .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn xft_get_nanos
returns the current time in nanoseconds, as a
.Vt t_u64a .
The value wraps after roughly 584 years of uptime.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Measure the duration of a region in nanoseconds.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#include <xft.h>

t_u64a	time_region(void (*fn)(void))
{
	t_u64a	start;
	t_u64a	stop;

	start = xft_get_nanos();
	fn();
	stop = xft_get_nanos();
	return (stop - start);
}
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xft_os 3 ,
.Xr xft_rng 3 ,
.Xr xft_rt 3 ,
.Xr xft 7
.Sh CAVEATS
The epoch is unspecified.
A difference of two values is meaningful; the values themselves are not.
.Pp
On x86_64 the returned time is derived from the cycle counter and inherits
its caveats: the value is only as accurate as the calibration, and it can
jump if the thread migrates between sockets whose counters are not
synchronised, or if the counter frequency changes between calibration and
measurement.
Pin the thread to a CPU when precise timing matters.
.Pp
The first call on x86_64 blocks for at least ten milliseconds while the
counter is calibrated.
Every later call is a counter read and a multiply.
.Pp
The initialization of the calibration state is not thread safe.
Two threads calling
.Fn xft_get_nanos
for the first time concurrently can both run the calibration; the resulting
value is still monotonic but may be less accurate.
.Pp
The return value is
.Vt t_u64a .
The internal expression on x86_64 casts the product to
.Vt t_u128
before shifting, so the multiplication does not overflow, but the final
result is truncated back to 64 bits.
